Task 7.10 — Isolated Five-Slot Set-Based Selection Prototype

OBJECTIVE

Test whether a complementary, set-based selection strategy can allocate the existing five evidence slots more effectively than the current sequential selector.

This is an isolated experimental prototype only.

The experiment MUST NOT change production behavior.

The experiment MUST NOT increase the final evidence limit.

The experiment MUST NOT replace or modify the reranker.

The experiment MUST NOT modify retrieval, RRF, candidate generation, eligibility, thresholds, evidence text, provenance, or downstream agents.

The only experimental change is HOW ALREADY-ELIGIBLE frozen candidates compete for the existing five evidence slots.

==================================================

FROZEN INPUTS
==================================================

Use ONLY the saved Task 7.9 selection-analysis artifacts and the frozen Task 7.8/7.8.1 results.

Use the same depth-50 cases:

validate:1
validate:2
validate:3

Use the already-saved candidate IDs, query text, source text, provenance, roles, reranker scores, eligibility decisions, ranking information, and selector traces.

Do NOT:

rerun MiniLM
rerun MedCPT
load another reranker
query Qdrant
rebuild BM25
regenerate embeddings
regenerate RRF
regenerate candidate pools
modify evidence text
modify queries
modify eligibility
read hidden DDXPlus labels
read benchmark labels

If the saved Task 7.9 artifacts are insufficient to reconstruct the required inputs exactly, STOP and report what is missing.

Before implementing the prototype, reproduce the existing Task 7.9 control selector exactly.

The control must match the saved selection for all six saved selections/conditions where applicable.

Verify:

selected evidence IDs
selected order
roles
document IDs
rejection events
final evidence count

If exact control reproduction fails, STOP.

Do not proceed using an approximate reconstruction.

Implement ONE isolated set-based selector prototype.

The prototype must preserve:

maximum final evidence count = 5
existing eligibility gate
existing candidate pool
existing reranker scores
existing evidence/provenance
existing document constraints
existing redundancy constraint
existing contextual constraints where already represented
existing two-pass/document constraints unless the experiment explicitly records a diagnostic alternative

The prototype changes only the selection strategy.

Do NOT invent disease-specific rules.

Do NOT add title-specific boosts.

Do NOT add asthma/panic/flu rules.

Do NOT tune directly to the three patient cases.

The experimental selector should select a complementary set rather than simply taking the highest-ranked individual passages.

For each eligible candidate, represent generic auditable features derived from the frozen query/source data.

Required dimensions:

A. DIRECT RELEVANCE

Prefer evidence that meaningfully addresses the observed findings or explicitly recorded history.

Do not reward simple shared words without meaningful correspondence.

B. NEW COVERAGE

Prefer candidates that add useful information not already represented by the currently selected set.

This must be measured as incremental/marginal coverage.

C. SECTION USEFULNESS

Prefer sections whose content is appropriate to the information need represented by the originating query.

Do not universally prefer "symptoms".

Do not universally prefer "treatment".

Section usefulness must remain generic and query-dependent.

D. CONTEXT COMPATIBILITY

Avoid rewarding passages that depend on:

an unestablished exposure
an unestablished subtype
an incompatible population
an adult-only context when inappropriate
circumstances not supported by the frozen patient observations

Do not infer a diagnosis.

E. SELF-CONTAINEDNESS

Prefer coherent passages that can be understood independently.

Do not reward fragmentary continuation chunks merely because they contain matching words.

Do NOT tune arbitrary numerical weights against the three cases.

Use a transparent deterministic feature representation.

If a scalar objective is required, use a clearly documented generic formulation in which:

direct relevance contributes positively
new coverage contributes positively
section usefulness contributes positively
context compatibility contributes positively
self-containedness contributes positively
redundancy contributes negatively
contextual mismatch contributes negatively

Existing reranker score may be included as ONE input feature.

Do NOT assume scores from different originating queries are directly comparable.

Document the exact normalization/comparison method used.

If the available frozen data does not support a defensible implementation of one of these dimensions, do not invent a proxy silently. Report the limitation and stop that part of the analysis.

Use a deterministic greedy marginal-gain procedure for the prototype:

Start with an empty selected set.
Consider all eligible candidates.
For each candidate, calculate its incremental usefulness relative to the current selected set.
Reject candidates that violate existing hard constraints.
Select the candidate with the highest documented marginal contribution.
Repeat until:
five candidates are selected, or
no eligible candidate can be added.

At every selection step record:

candidate ID
current selected IDs
direct relevance contribution
new-coverage contribution
section usefulness
context compatibility
self-containedness
redundancy penalty
contextual mismatch penalty
reranker contribution, if used
total marginal score
constraints checked
candidates rejected at that step
reason for rejection

Tie-breaking MUST be deterministic and documented.

Preserve the existing two-per-document maximum.

Do NOT automatically select the first two highest-ranked passages from a document.

Instead, allow the set-based procedure to compare the marginal contribution of eligible passages from the same document.

A document may contribute up to two passages only when those passages satisfy the existing constraints and provide complementary value.

Do not remove the document cap.

Do not increase it.

Preserve the existing Jaccard redundancy rule and threshold.

Do not weaken the threshold.

Do not remove the rule.

When a candidate is rejected because of redundancy, record:

candidate ID
selected passage causing the redundancy conflict
measured overlap
rejection reason

Do NOT use the old irreversible schedule:

history first → current findings → history backfill

for the experimental selector.

Instead, current and historical/background evidence should compete jointly within the final five-slot set.

If a history/background representation is required as a hard constraint by the existing architecture, preserve that requirement as a final-set constraint rather than selecting the first history winner irreversibly.

Do not invent a new role priority.

Record role composition of the final set.

Inspect the following using the saved Task 7.8/7.9 traces.

A. PANIC DISORDER

The useful Panic Disorder evidence became eligible under MedCPT but was excluded by the existing selector.

Determine whether the set-based prototype selects it while maintaining the five-slot limit.

Record:

whether selected
which passage it replaces, if any
why the prototype considers it complementary
whether any existing constraint prevents selection

Do not call this a diagnosis.

B. FLU

Verify that the Flu evidence remains selected.

If it changes, explain exactly why.

C. FAINTING

Verify whether the existing selected Fainting evidence remains selected.

Any change must be explicitly traced.

D. ASTHMA

Inspect:

asthma symptoms
asthma definition
asthma diagnostic/evaluation/background passages

Determine which are:

eligible
selected by control
selected by prototype
displaced
rejected by constraints

E. OLDER ADULT MENTAL HEALTH

Inspect whether the prototype continues to select or reject it and why.

Do not make a clinical correctness judgment.

Use ONLY the already completed Task 7.8.1 A/B/C classifications.

Do not create new relevance labels.

