Do NOT define success as simply selecting more evidence.

Do NOT define success as selecting all A-rated passages.

The prototype is informative only if it demonstrates a more complementary allocation within the same five-slot budget while preserving useful existing selections and avoiding indiscriminate admission of weak candidates.

Specifically inspect:

whether Panic Disorder can be recovered within five slots;
whether Flu remains selected;
whether Fainting remains selected;
whether useful asthma evidence is preserved;
whether the seven C-rated examples are selectively excluded;
whether the selector remains auditable and deterministic.

Do NOT claim clinical accuracy.

There are only three frozen cases.

Do NOT tune weights, thresholds, or feature formulas until the desired outcome is achieved.

If a design choice appears uncertain, document it rather than iteratively tuning it.

One prototype only.

No second prototype.

No parameter sweep.

No optimization against A/B/C labels.

